 2. SpaceX plans nationwide cell network 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Annelise Capossela/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Elon Musk's SpaceX is moving ahead with plans to build a nationwide cellular network, selecting Qualcomm to provide key technology for tens of thousands of cell sites that it plans to begin deploying next year.

 Why it matters: SpaceX's move threatens to upend the established wireless industry, though its ultimate ambition may be bigger than simply stealing smartphone subscribers from Verizon, AT&amp;T and T-Mobile.

 Musk has the financial resources, satellite network and growing portfolio of wireless spectrum to mount a serious challenge to the status quo. Still, building a network to rival the established carriers — and persuading their customers to switch — remains a daunting and potentially expensive undertaking. Driving the news: Qualcomm said today that SpaceX has selected its Dragonwing chips and software to help power Starlink Mobile's planned nationwide terrestrial 5G network.

 The companies have a multiyear agreement covering tens of thousands of traditional cellular sites, with deployment expected to begin in 2027, per Qualcomm. Qualcomm says it will provide the chips and foundational software for radio and baseband processing, while SpaceX will develop additional software and other elements of the network. Between the lines: SpaceX's ambitions likely go beyond competing for smartphone subscribers, with the company potentially targeting emerging markets for AI-powered devices and robots.

 For example, a satellite network could provide more continuous outdoor connectivity for package-delivery drones, security robots, construction robots and autonomous delivery vehicles. By the numbers: Joe Madden, principal analyst at Mobile Experts, estimates SpaceX will spend at least $100 billion building out its combined satellite and terrestrial wireless network, including the cost of acquiring spectrum.

